 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: each browser holds a socket to the board's servers and receives changes as they commit. Updates arrive well inside the freshness goal, without a realtime vendor or provider coupling.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around that choice. Change storage keeps ordered, replayable event revisions and reliably publishes each committed revision. Delivery manages socket sessions and connection capacity. Browser sync detects dropped connections, reconnects, and catches up from its last known revision. The system owns connection-capacity and reconnect operations itself, as the cost of fast updates without provider coupling.
 
